(Bug #88: `Print not <text>` segfaulted, and so did a list and a map - the
 list printing `[` and the map `{` first. `infer_expr_type` and
 `is_float_expr` in codegen/expr.rs returned the OPERAND's type for a unary
 `not`, so Print was told a boolean was text and emitted a text print of the
 boolean 0 - dereferencing address 0. A float operand made `not f` print
 `0.0`, a float where a boolean belongs. A `not` answers a boolean whatever
 it is applied to - the rule `prescan_expr_tag` in codegen/tags.rs already
 states in so many words - so every row below prints 0 or 1.

 What this file pins is the TYPE, which is what #88 was: a `not` is a
 boolean, and no operand makes Print dereference it. The VALUE on a
 pointer-typed operand is a null-pointer test, not an emptiness test -
 `not ""` and `not []` both answer 0 because the pointer is real, the same
 shape #33 fixed for `is empty`. Nothing in LANGUAGE.md gives `not` an
 emptiness reading, so the rows below record what the compiler does; use
 `is empty` to ask about contents.

 The `value` and mixed-list-element rows are controls: `infer_expr_type`
 answers None for both, so Print already fell back to the runtime tag, which
 codegen/tags.rs has tagged TAG_BOOLEAN all along. They were safe before the
 fix and are here to keep the dynamic path honest alongside the static one.)

a text called greeting is "hi".
Print not greeting.

a text called blank is "".
Print not blank.

a list called readings is [1, 2, 3].
Print not readings.

a list called no_readings is [].
Print not no_readings.

a map called stock is {"apples": 1}.
Print not stock.

a float called ratio is 2.5.
Print not ratio.

a buffer called scratch is 16 bytes in size.
Print not scratch.

a number called depth is 20.
Print not depth.

a number called nought is 0.
Print not nought.

a boolean called ready is true.
Print not ready.

a boolean called done is false.
Print not done.

a value called payload is "hi".
Print not payload.

a list called mixed is [1, "two", 3.5].
Print not element 2 of mixed.

Print "a not in a format string: {not greeting}".
